{
  "requirement": "When authoring evidence for a requirement, its complete fenced declaration MUST\nbe included in the mandatory input set defined by\n{cite}`requirements.authoring.mandatory_inputs`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict:** the subject is met. The implementation is complete and marked, and the tests cover the obligation. One test gap is minor.\n\n**Obligation.** For a requirement under assessment, its complete fenced declaration must be in the mandatory input set.\n\n**Implementation.** `AssessmentSources::prepare` in `src/authoring/inputs.rs` carries the `{impl}` marker for `requirements.authoring_input`.\n- It first checks that the subject is a requirement or named example and not a term.\n- It seeds the selector list with `InputSelector::Requirements { requirements: subject_name }`, so the subject comes first and is always included.\n- `source_input` calls `selector.resolve`. In `src/inputs.rs` this emits the matched definition's `raw_lines`, which are the full fenced declaration.\n- The subject is deduplicated through `insert_selector`. It is not removed by `generated_evidence_selector`, which only filters file-range and Rust-item selectors under `evidence/`.\n- The remaining behavior in `prepare` is cited to other requirements it implements: cited requirements, impl/test references, definitions, and read denial. It is not unmarked extra behavior.\n\n**Tests.** Two tests carry `{test}` markers for the requirement.\n- `bundle_combines_direct_references_terms_and_current_existing_selectors` builds a bundle for `requirements.ready`.\n  - It checks that the bundle contains referenced declarations, including the example `requirements.case` and the cited `requirements.base`.\n  - It checks that the uncited `requirements.distant` is excluded.\n  - It checks that earlier evidence inputs are not carried forward.\n  - For the example subject `requirements.case`, it asserts that `mandatory_inputs()[0][\"requirements\"] == \"requirements.case\"`, so the subject's own declaration is the first input.\n- `mandatory_git_inputs_require_staging_declarations_sources_and_terms` shows that the file holding the declaration (`md/requirements.md`) is a required readable input. `prepare` fails with a `git add` error when that file is unstaged and succeeds once it is staged.\n\n**Minor gap.** No test asserts directly that the requirement-kind subject (`requirements.ready`) appears as an input. That path is the same code that the example-kind assertion exercises, and the initial selector is unconditional. This is not enough to fail the requirement.",
